                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Sunset's Ashes is a very short visual novel, but it is one of those games where I think the length actually works in its favour. I play as Alex while she climbs a mountain with Nic, who has never really been comfortable with this kind of trip before. The two of them spend the climb talking about their lives and remembering things from their relationship, so most of the game is about getting to know them rather than doing anything complicated. There is no combat or big gameplay system here. It is mostly reading, making a few choices and watching their relationship unfold.\n\nThe thing I like most is the atmosphere. The mountain setting gives the whole story a quiet feeling that works really well with the conversations. I am moving through different parts of the climb while Alex and Nic talk about their past, and there is something quite nice about how ordinary their conversations can feel. It does not make them sound like perfect fictional characters who always know exactly what to say. They feel more like two people who have spent a long time together and are trying to make the most of an important day. That makes the quieter moments stand out more when the tone starts changing.\n\nI also like that I can choose different paths during the climb. The choices do not completely turn it into a huge branching adventure, but they give me different pieces of information about Alex and Nic and make another playthrough worthwhile. I can take different routes up the mountain and see extra dialogue between them, which is a nice way of handling replayability in such a short game. I would have liked a little more interaction, but I think adding loads of systems would have distracted from what the game is actually trying to do.\n\nThe biggest problem for me is the length. Sunset's Ashes is very short, and once I have seen the story there is not a huge amount left for me to do. The different paths help, but they are still small variations on the same basic climb. There are also no voice performances, so I am spending most of the time reading rather than interacting with the world. That is completely fine for a visual novel like this, but it does mean I have to be interested in the characters or I will probably finish it feeling like there was not much there.\n\nThe visuals are simple, but I actually think they fit the game. The mountain environments are peaceful and the artwork gives the story enough personality without trying too hard. I also like the music because it helps the quieter scenes feel more emotional without constantly telling me how I am supposed to feel. The game is clearly not trying to compete with a large visual novel in terms of production quality. It is much more interested in creating a small moment between two people, and I think that approach works better than trying to make everything bigger.\n\nWhat makes the game work for me is that it stays focused. I am not dealing with pointless side systems, long tutorials or filler sections. I am just following two people up a mountain and finding out why this particular climb matters so much to them. There are nine achievements, and the different paths give me a small reason to replay the story, but I would not pretend that this is a game I am going to spend dozens of hours with. It is more like a short story that happens to be interactive, and I think treating it that way makes the experience much easier to appreciate.\n\nOverall, Sunset's Ashes is simple, short and quite effective at what it is trying to do. I like the relationship between Alex and Nic, the quiet mountain setting and the way the different paths reveal more about them without making the story unnecessarily complicated. The lack of gameplay variety and very short length hold it back for me, because there is only so much I can get out of a few different routes. Still, it is completely free, it has a clear idea and it manages to make a small story feel meaningful without dragging it out. For me, that is enough to make it worth playing.\n\nRating: 7/10\n",
